Incident note, 7 August 2026

Continuous integration was red for two commits and nobody noticed, because the person who broke it checked the wrong workflow. The documentation deploy was green and was reported as success; the test workflow was failing the whole time.

The break came from adding identifiers to headings so the documentation site could have anchors and a table of contents. One Go test asserted the exact old markup and was fixed. The mistake was fixing only the test that failed locally instead of searching the repository for every place that pattern appeared.

Two more sites existed. The MCP server test asserted the old heading markup and failed in CI. More seriously, the C++ SDK has its own HTML renderer that an earlier survey missed, because that survey was looking specifically for chart rendering and the conclusion was over generalised. So for two commits there were three renderers producing two different behaviours, and the C++ job stayed green the entire time because its test asserted the old form.

All three renderers now emit identical anchors including the deduplication suffix, checked by rendering the same input through each rather than trusting the tests.

The local test matrix was also missing two jobs that CI runs, which is why this survived as long as it did. Both were added.

